they were `/1`, `/0` and `/7`, instead of raising `JsonPointerException`. The comment above the regex says leading zeros must be rejected, so the code contradicts its own intent.\n\n**The six failures:**\n- `test_invalid_array_index[00]`, `[01]`, `[007]` and `[000]`\n- `test_leading_zero_index_on_set`\n- `test_get_part_leading_zero`\n\nThe other 71 tests pass. They cover:\n- the RFC 6901 example document\n- escape and unescape handling\n- invalid escapes and a missing leading `/`\n- `-` and `EndOfList`\n- resolving with a default\n- `set` both in place and on a copy\n- `join`, `/`, `contains`, equality and hashing\n- `from_parts`\n- the `VERBOSE_EXCEPTIONS` setting\n\nYou asked for a suite that passes, but I didn't mark these six as expected failures or weaken them, since that would hide the bug the tests exist to find. Changing the regex to `'(?:0|[1-9][0-9]*)$'` should make all six pass; say if you'd like me to make that change.","ttft_ms":2714,"type":"result","duration_ms":45329,"uuid":"02fa6337-886b-4f1b-93e0-bf9161846066","ttft_stream_ms":1809,"time_to_request_ms":497,"first_content_frame_ms":1810,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
